#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069630} :activity/id #uuid "ad5bc873-c059-5e29-a890-6a3c7109ea97" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048705 :topic/id #uuid "b6d4a471-5c94-57bb-ac87-9fd114198d79" :topic/math-academy-id 424} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069630 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186070032 :tutorial/content "In some cases, applying integration by parts can lead to a cyclic pattern, where the original integral reappears after a few steps. This cyclic behavior allows us to solve the integral by forming an equation that can be solved algebraically.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the following example:\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x} \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet's try using the method of integration by parts. We suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}x & \\,\\Rightarrow \\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,\\Rightarrow \\,v=\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{ln}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}x(\\operatorname{ln}x)-\\int (\\operatorname{ln}x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}x-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}x}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={ln}^{2}x-I\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the same integral $I$ on both sides of the equality. Thinking about $I$ as some unknown variable, we can easily solve this equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={ln}^{2}x-I \\\\ I+I & ={ln}^{2}x \\\\ 2I & ={ln}^{2}x \\\\ I & =\\frac{{ln}^{2}x}{2}+C\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we added a constant of integration right at the very end of the calculation." :tutorial/id #uuid "eac7b2d2-bdab-56e5-bb03-00e69bb0caf6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 904 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ebba75b0-acff-